  "prediction": "The report discusses the Fire Management Assistance Grants (FMAGs) under the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief Act. FMAGs provide federal assistance for fire suppression activities on non-federal lands, authorized by the FEMA Regional Administrators. States or tribes can request FMAGs when fires threaten to become major disasters. The request should include cost estimates and information about the size and location of the fire. Once issued, FMAGs cover 75% of eligible fire suppression costs, with the remaining 25% being covered by the state or tribe. FMAGs can be requested at any time, and under the Sandy Recovery Act of 2013, tribes have equal standing to request FMAGs as states. The report answers frequently asked questions about the FMAG process, including how to request assistance, the evaluation criteria, and the types of assistance provided. FMAGs are funded through FEMA's Disaster Relief Fund and do not affect the availability of other disaster assistance or reduce the balance in the fund. The report also explains that fires on federal lands are generally not eligible for FMAG assistance, but there are exceptions for fires on mixed federal and non-federal lands, where the state has a cooperative agreement with the federal agency for suppression activities and is not reimbursed under another federal statute.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the Fire Management Assistance Grants (FMAGs) under the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief Act. FMAGs provide federal assistance for fire suppression activities on non-federal lands, authorized by the FEMA Regional Administrators. States or tribes can request FMAGs when fires threaten to become major disasters. The request should include cost estimates and information about the size and location of the fire. Once issued, FMAGs cover 75% of eligible fire suppression costs, with the remaining 25% being covered by the state or tribe. FMAGs can be requested at any time, and under the Sandy Recovery Act of 2013, tribes have equal standing to request FMAGs as states. The report answers frequently asked questions about the FMAG process, including how to request assistance, the evaluation criteria, and the types of assistance provided. FMAGs are funded through FEMA's Disaster Relief Fund and do not affect the availability of other disaster assistance or reduce the balance in the fund. The report also explains that fires on federal lands are generally not eligible for FMAG assistance, but there are exceptions for fires on mixed federal and non-federal lands, where the state has a cooperative agreement with the federal agency for suppression activities and is not reimbursed under another federal statute.",
  "score": 36.2831853483828,
  "answers": [
    "Section 420 of the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (P.L. 93-288, hereinafter the Stafford Act) authorizes the President to \"declare\" a Fire Management Assistance Grant (FMAG). In the interest of saving time, the authority to make the declaration has been delegated to the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA's) Regional Administrators. Once issued, the FMAG declaration authorizes various forms of federal fire suppression assistance such as the provision of equipment, personnel, and grants to state, local, and tribal governments for the control, management, and mitigation of any fire on certain public or private forest land or grassland that might become a major disaster. This federal assistance requires a cost-sharing component such that state, local, and tribal governments are responsible for 25% of the expenses. This report answers frequently asked questions about FMAGs. This report will be updated as events warrant."
  ],
